metadata:
  name: Sorbetto al Limone Intenso
  images:
    - https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Lemon_sorbet_%284173840081%29.jpg/960px-Lemon_sorbet_%284173840081%29.jpg
  description: "Riviera Lemon Sorbet. Riviera-style sorbetto al limone — sharp, perfumed, and unapologetically lemony, built on a heavy zest infusion."
  notes: "Needs an ice cream machine. The juice goes in raw and cold at the end, never heated."
  types: [Dessert, Ice Cream]
  estimatedPrepTime: 30 min + 4 h
  servings: 6

ingredients:
 - 300 ml freshly squeezed lemon juice (about 6-8 lemons)
 - 230 g granulated sugar
 - 400 ml water
 - 5 st lemons, zested (zest only, no white pith)
 - 0.1 tsp fine salt

actions:
 - divider Make the base
 - free_text Scrub the lemons in warm water.
 - free_text Zest all 5 st lemons into a saucepan with a fine grater or peeler, taking only the yellow surface, no white pith.
 - free_text Juice as many lemons as needed to get 300 ml freshly squeezed lemon juice, then refrigerate the juice.
 - free_text Add 230 g granulated sugar and 400 ml water to the saucepan with the zest.
 - free_text Warm over medium heat, stirring, just until the sugar dissolves — do not simmer.
 - free_text Remove from heat, cover, and steep for 30 minutes as it cools.
 - free_text Strain the syrup, pressing firmly on the zest to wring out the oils, then discard the zest. The syrup should smell intensely of lemon peel.
 - free_text Once the syrup is at room temperature or cooler, stir in the cold juice and 0.1 tsp fine salt.
 - free_text Taste and adjust with more sugar or a splash more juice — it should be bracingly lemony and slightly too sweet when cold.
 - divider Chill, churn and freeze
 - free_text Refrigerate the base until completely cold, at least 120 minutes or overnight.
 - free_text Churn until soft-serve consistency, typically 20–25 minutes. It can be served straight from the machine.
 - free_text If not serving immediately, transfer to a chilled container, press parchment against the surface, and freeze until scoopable, about 120 minutes.
